\subsection*{Abstract}
This journal entry formalizes the theoretical structure of Codex
Capsules as immutable provenance anchors within the hybrid verification
architecture. Capsules serve as cryptographically committed,
dynamically stable, and logically validated containers for mathematical
discoveries, simulation invariants, and system-level transmissions.
Their role is expanded today to include direct integration with the
Proof Validation Pipeline (PVP) and the COSE Lyapunov stability engine.

\subsection*{1. Conceptual Overview}
A Codex Capsule $\mathcal{C}$ is defined as a triplet:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}
= (\mathcal{M}, H_{\mathrm{commit}}, \Gamma),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathcal{M}$ is the manuscript payload (mathematical,
          dynamical, or architectural content),
    \item $H_{\mathrm{commit}}$ is the cryptographic commitment hash,
    \item $\Gamma$ is the glyph-seal semantic layer.
\end{itemize}

Capsules are immutable, timestamped, and attributed. They form the
permanent registry of validated discoveries.

\subsection*{2. Commitment Hash Structure}
The commitment hash is defined as:
\begin{equation}
H_{\mathrm{commit}}
= \mathrm{SHA256}(\mathcal{M} \parallel t \parallel \mathrm{author}),
\end{equation}
ensuring:
\begin{itemize}
    \item immutability,
    \item provenance integrity,
    \item resistance to retroactive modification.
\end{itemize}

This hash is the anchor point linking the capsule to the Codex registry.

\subsection*{3. Glyph-Seal Semantics}
Each capsule contains a glyph-seal $\Gamma$ encoding:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item semantic classification,
    \item dynamical resonance (COSE),
    \item verification lineage (PVP),
    \item designer attribution.
\end{enumerate}

Glyph-seals function as symbolic invariants.  
They are not decorative; they are structural markers of capsule identity.

\subsection*{4. Capsule Stability Under Hybrid Dynamics}
Capsules are validated against the Lyapunov functional:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}(X)
= (X - X^\star)^\top Q (X - X^\star)
+ \sum_{k} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(
\pi_k(X) \,\big\|\, \pi_k^\star
\right).
\end{equation}

A capsule is accepted only if:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}(X) \leq 0,
\end{equation}
ensuring dynamical stability of the manuscript payload.

If:
\begin{equation}
\dot{\mathcal{V}}(X) > 0,
\end{equation}
the capsule is rejected and neutralized.

\subsection*{5. Integration with the Proof Validation Pipeline}
Capsules now serve as the terminal objects of the PVP.  
A proof $\Pi$ is considered fully validated only when encapsulated into
a Codex Capsule $\mathcal{C}$.

The acceptance predicate is:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{PVP}}(\Pi, \psi) = 1
\quad \Longrightarrow \quad
\exists \mathcal{C} : H_{\mathrm{commit}}(\mathcal{C}) = H(\Pi).
\end{equation}

Thus, capsules become the immutable end-state of validated logic.

